{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QcT1z85roW82VGPtiJHbij","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (41.1KB). Full output saved to: /home/scheschb/.claude/projects/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-qM4rJt/d13ca368-a34f-4ebf-a098-8de9fa940614/tool-results/b2e5rg3uf.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n     1\t//! Rust translation of `c_src/src/lib.c` — a cute_c2-style 2D GJK distance\n     2\t//! solver, built as a C shared library.\n     3\t//!\n     4\t//! This crate reproduces the **complete public ABI** of the C library (all 31\n     5\t//! exported symbols) and is bit-for-bit behaviour compatible with it,\n     6\t//! including its quirks and bugs.\n     7\t//!\n     8\t//! # Why the arithmetic looks the way it does\n     9\t//!\n    10\t//! The C library is built as a `-fPIC` shared object **without**\n    11\t//! `-fvisibility=hidden` and with no `static` functions, so every helper is\n    12\t//! interposable and GCC cannot inline any of them — the disassembly shows that\n    13\t//! every single call (`c2V`, `c2Dot`, `c2Sub`, `c2Add`, …) goes through the\n    14\t//! PLT. That means the library's entire floating-point behaviour is determined\n    15\t//! by a small, enumerable set of SSE scalar/packed instructions inside the\n    16\t//! leaf functions.\n    17\t//!\n    18\t//! For every *finite* input, IEEE-754 makes those results uniquely determined,\n    19\t//! so a straightforward translation is already bit-exact. The one place where\n    20\t//! \"obvious\" Rust diverges is **which NaN survives** an operation: `addss`,\n    21\t//! `mulss`, `subss` and `divss` return the *destination* operand's NaN in\n    22\t//! preference to the source operand's, and GCC's register allocator sometimes\n    23\t//! commutes `addss`/`mulss` relative to the C source order (LLVM does too, but\n    24\t//! makes different choices). Because a NaN's sign bit and payload are then\n    25\t//! observable in the output, this module routes all arithmetic through\n    26\t//! `add_l`/`add_r`/`mul_l`/`mul_r`/`sub_l`/`div_l` helpers that pin the\n    27\t//! destination operand to exactly what GCC emitted at each site. The choices\n    28\t//! are documented per call site against the disassembly.\n    29\t\n    30\t#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n    31\t#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]\n    32\t#![allow(non_upper_case_globals)]\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d13ca368-a34f-4ebf-a098-8de9fa940614","uuid":"dd263ee5-3153-4eb1-a6e1-0acee07364a3","timestamp":"2026-09-05T08:05:22.392Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t//! Rust translation of `c_src/src/lib.c` — a cute_c2-style 2D GJK distance\n     2\t//! solver, built as a C shared library.\n     3\t//!\n     4\t//! This crate reproduces the **complete public ABI** of the C library (all 31\n     5\t//! exported symbols) and is bit-for-bit behaviour compatible with it,\n     6\t//! including its quirks and bugs.\n     7\t//!\n     8\t//! # Why the arithmetic looks the way it does\n     9\t//!\n    10\t//! The C library is built as a `-fPIC` shared object **without**\n    11\t//! `-fvisibility=hidden` and with no `static` functions, so every helper is\n    12\t//! interposable and GCC cannot inline any of them — the disassembly shows that\n    13\t//! every single call (`c2V`, `c2Dot`, `c2Sub`, `c2Add`, …) goes through the\n    14\t//! PLT. That means the library's entire floating-point behaviour is determined\n    15\t//! by a small, enumerable set of SSE scalar/packed instructions inside the\n    16\t//! leaf functions.\n    17\t//!\n    18\t//! For every *finite* input, IEEE-754 makes those results uniquely determined,\n    19\t//! so a straightforward translation is already bit-exact. The one place where\n    20\t//! \"obvious\" Rust diverges is **which NaN survives** an operation: `addss`,\n    21\t//! `mulss`, `subss` and `divss` return the *destination* operand's NaN in\n    22\t//! preference to the source operand's, and GCC's register allocator sometimes\n    23\t//! commutes `addss`/`mulss` relative to the C source order (LLVM does too, but\n    24\t//! makes different choices). Because a NaN's sign bit and payload are then\n    25\t//! observable in the output, this module routes all arithmetic through\n    26\t//! `add_l`/`add_r`/`mul_l`/`mul_r`/`sub_l`/`div_l` helpers that pin the\n    27\t//! destination operand to exactly what GCC emitted at each site. The choices\n    28\t//! are documented per call site against the disassembly.\n    29\t\n    30\t#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n    31\t#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]\n    32\t#![allow(non_upper_case_globals)]\n    33\t#![allow(unused_assignments)]\n    34\t\n    35\tuse core::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};\n    36\tuse core::ptr::{addr_of, addr_of_mut, null, null_mut};\n    37\t\n    38\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    39\t// Constants, spelled out as literals in the C source\n    40\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    41\t\n    42\t/// `FLT_MAX`\n    43\tconst FLT_MAX: f32 = 3.402_823_466_385_288_598_117_041_834_845_169_25e+38_f32;\n    44\t/// `FLT_EPSILON`\n    45\tconst FLT_EPSILON: f32 = 1.192_092_895_507_812_5e-7_f32;\n    46\t\n    47\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    48\t// SSE-exact scalar arithmetic\n    49\t//\n    50\t// Intel SDM, ADDSS/SUBSS/MULSS/DIVSS `xmm1, xmm2`:\n    51\t//   * if the destination operand (`xmm1`) is NaN, the result is that NaN,\n    52\t//     quieted (SNaN -> QNaN by setting the mantissa MSB); sign and payload\n    53\t//     are otherwise preserved;\n    54\t//   * otherwise, if the source operand is NaN, the result is that NaN,\n    55\t//     quieted;\n    56\t//   * otherwise the IEEE-754 operation is performed (which itself may raise\n    57\t//     #I and produce the \"QNaN indefinite\" for e.g. 0*Inf or Inf-Inf — that\n    58\t//     case is order independent, so plain Rust arithmetic reproduces it).\n    59\t//\n    60\t// `_l` means the *left* argument was GCC's destination operand; `_r` means\n    61\t// GCC commuted the instruction so the *right* argument is the destination.\n    62\t// `SUBSS`/`DIVSS` are not commutative, so only the `_l` form exists.\n    63\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    64\t\n    65\t/// Quiet a NaN exactly the way an SSE arithmetic instruction does: set the\n    66\t/// mantissa MSB, leave sign and the rest of the payload untouched.\n    67\t#[inline(always)]\n    68\tfn quiet(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    69\t    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n    70\t}\n    71\t\n    72\t/// IEEE-754 negation: a pure sign-bit flip (`xorps` against `0x80000000`),\n    73\t/// which never quiets a NaN. This is what GCC emits for `-x` in `c2Neg`,\n    74\t/// `c2Skew` and `c2CCW90`.\n    75\t#[inline(always)]\n    76\tfn fneg(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    77\t    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() ^ 0x8000_0000)\n    78\t}\n    79\t\n    80\t/// `addss a, b` — destination is `a`.\n    81\t#[inline(always)]\n    82\tfn add_l(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    83\t    if a.is_nan() {\n    84\t        quiet(a)\n    85\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n    86\t        quiet(b)\n    87\t    } else {\n    88\t        a + b\n    89\t    }\n    90\t}\n    91\t\n    92\t/// `addss b, a` — destination is `b` (GCC commuted the operands).\n    93\t#[inline(always)]\n    94\tfn add_r(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    95\t    if b.is_nan() {\n    96\t        quiet(b)\n    97\t    } else if a.is_nan() {\n    98\t        quiet(a)\n    99\t    } else {\n   100\t        a + b\n   101\t    }\n   102\t}\n   103\t\n   104\t/// `subss a, b` — destination is `a` (subtraction is never commuted).\n   105\t#[inline(always)]\n   106\tfn sub_l(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n   107\t    if a.is_nan() {\n   108\t        quiet(a)\n   109\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n   110\t        quiet(b)\n   111\t    } else {\n   112\t        a - b\n   113\t    }\n   114\t}\n   115\t\n   116\t/// `mulss a, b` — destination is `a`.\n   117\t#[inline(always)]\n   118\tfn mul_l(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n   119\t    if a.is_nan() {\n   120\t        quiet(a)\n   121\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n   122\t        quiet(b)\n   123\t    } else {\n   124\t        a * b\n   125\t    }\n   126\t}\n   127\t\n   128\t/// `mulss b, a` — destination is `b` (GCC commuted the operands).\n   129\t#[inline(always)]\n   130\tfn mul_r(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n   131\t    if b.is_nan() {\n   132\t        quiet(b)\n   133\t    } else if a.is_nan() {\n   134\t        quiet(a)\n   135\t    } else {\n   136\t        a * b\n   137\t    }\n   138\t}\n   139\t\n   140\t/// `divss a, b` — destination is `a` (division is never commuted).\n   141\t#[inline(always)]\n   142\tfn div_l(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n   143\t    if a.is_nan() {\n   144\t        quiet(a)\n   145\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n   146\t        quiet(b)\n   147\t    } else {\n   148\t        a / b\n   149\t    }\n   150\t}\n   151\t\n   152\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   153\t// C2_TYPE — an unfixed C enum, so `int`-sized in the ABI\n   154\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   155\t\n   156\tpub const C2_TYPE_CIRCLE: c_int = 0;\n   157\tpub const C2_TYPE_AABB: c_int = 1;\n   158\tpub const C2_TYPE_CAPSULE: c_int = 2;\n   159\t\n   160\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   161\t// Structures — layouts mirror the C definitions exactly\n   162\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   163\t\n   164\t/// `typedef struct c2v { float x; float y; } c2v;`\n   165\t#[repr(C)]\n   166\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   167\tpub struct c2v {\n   168\t    pub x: f32,\n   169\t    pub y: f32,\n   170\t}\n   171\t\n   172\t/// `typedef struct c2r { float c; float s; } c2r;`\n   173\t#[repr(C)]\n   174\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   175\tpub struct c2r {\n   176\t    pub c: f32,\n   177\t    pub s: f32,\n   178\t}\n   179\t\n   180\t/// `typedef struct c2x { c2v p; c2r r; } c2x;`\n   181\t#[repr(C)]\n   182\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   183\tpub struct c2x {\n   184\t    pub p: c2v,\n   185\t    pub r: c2r,\n   186\t}\n   187\t\n   188\t/// `typedef struct c2Circle { c2v p; float r; } c2Circle;`\n   189\t#[repr(C)]\n   190\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   191\tpub struct c2Circle {\n   192\t    pub p: c2v,\n   193\t    pub r: f32,\n   194\t}\n   195\t\n   196\t/// `typedef struct c2AABB { c2v min; c2v max; } c2AABB;`\n   197\t#[repr(C)]\n   198\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   199\tpub struct c2AABB {\n   200\t    pub min: c2v,\n   201\t    pub max: c2v,\n   202\t}\n   203\t\n   204\t/// `typedef struct c2Capsule { c2v a; c2v b; float r; } c2Capsule;`\n   205\t#[repr(C)]\n   206\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   207\tpub struct c2Capsule {\n   208\t    pub a: c2v,\n   209\t    pub b: c2v,\n   210\t    pub r: f32,\n   211\t}\n   212\t\n   213\t/// ```c\n   214\t/// typedef struct c2GJKCache {\n   215\t///         float metric; int count; int iA[3]; int iB[3]; float div;\n   216\t/// } c2GJKCache;\n   217\t/// ```\n   218\t#[repr(C)]\n   219\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   220\tpub struct c2GJKCache {\n   221\t    pub metric: f32,\n   222\t    pub count: c_int,\n   223\t    pub iA: [c_int; 3],\n   224\t    pub iB: [c_int; 3],\n   225\t    pub div: f32,\n   226\t}\n   227\t\n   228\t/// `typedef struct { float radius; int count; c2v verts[8]; } c2Proxy;`\n   229\t#[repr(C)]\n   230\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   231\tpub struct c2Proxy {\n   232\t    pub radius: f32,\n   233\t    pub count: c_int,\n   234\t    pub verts: [c2v; 8],\n   235\t}\n   236\t\n   237\t/// `typedef struct { c2v sA; c2v sB; c2v p; float u; int iA; int iB; } c2sv;`\n   238\t#[repr(C)]\n   239\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   240\tpub struct c2sv {\n   241\t    pub sA: c2v,\n   242\t    pub sB: c2v,\n   243\t    pub p: c2v,\n   244\t    pub u: f32,\n   245\t    pub iA: c_int,\n   246\t    pub iB: c_int,\n   247\t}\n   248\t\n   249\t/// `typedef struct { c2sv a, b, c, d; float div; int count; } c2Simplex;`\n   250\t///\n   251\t/// The four `c2sv` members `a`, `b`, `c`, `d` are modelled as one array so the\n   252\t/// C idiom `c2sv *verts = &s->a; verts[i]` can be reproduced verbatim. The\n   253\t/// memory layout is byte-identical to the C struct.\n   254\t#[repr(C)]\n   255\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug)]\n   256\tpub struct c2Simplex {\n   257\t    /// `a`, `b`, `c`, `d`\n   258\t    pub verts: [c2sv; 4],\n   259\t    pub div: f32,\n   260\t    pub count: c_int,\n   261\t}\n   262\t\n   263\t// Compile-time layout assertions guaranteeing ABI equality with the C structs.\n   264\tconst _: () = {\n   265\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2v>() == 8 && core::mem::align_of::<c2v>() == 4);\n   266\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2r>() == 8);\n   267\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2x>() == 16);\n   268\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2Circle>() == 12);\n   269\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2AABB>() == 16);\n   270\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2Capsule>() == 20);\n   271\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2GJKCache>() == 36);\n   272\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2Proxy>() == 72);\n   273\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2sv>() == 36);\n   274\t    assert!(core::mem::size_of::<c2Simplex>() == 152);\n   275\t};\n   276\t\n   277\tconst ZERO_V: c2v = c2v { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 };\n   278\t\n   279\timpl c2Simplex {\n   280\t    #[inline]\n   281\t    const fn zeroed() -> c2Simplex {\n   282\t        const ZSV: c2sv = c2sv {\n   283\t            sA: ZERO_V,\n   284\t            sB: ZERO_V,\n   285\t            p: ZERO_V,\n   286\t            u: 0.0,\n   287\t            iA: 0,\n   288\t            iB: 0,\n   289\t        };\n   290\t        c2Simplex {\n   291\t            verts: [ZSV; 4],\n   292\t            div: 0.0,\n   293\t            count: 0,\n   294\t        }\n   295\t    }\n   296\t}\n   297\t\n   298\timpl c2Proxy {\n   299\t    #[inline]\n   300\t    const fn zeroed() -> c2Proxy {\n   301\t        c2Proxy {\n   302\t            radius: 0.0,\n   303\t            count: 0,\n   304\t            verts: [ZERO_V; 8],\n   305\t        }\n   306\t    }\n   307\t}\n   308\t\n   309\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   310\t// Vector / rotation helpers\n   311\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   312\t\n   313\t/// `c2v c2V(float x, float y)`\n   314\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   315\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2V(x: f32, y: f32) -> c2v {\n   316\t    let mut a = ZERO_V;\n   317\t    a.x = x;\n   318\t    a.y = y;\n   319\t    a\n   320\t}\n   321\t\n   322\t/// `c2v c2Mulvs(c2v a, float b)`\n   323\t///\n   324\t/// GCC broadcasts the scalar (`movsldup`) into the destination register and\n   325\t/// multiplies by `a`: `mulps %xmm2(a), %xmm0(b,b)`. The destination is\n   326\t/// therefore `b`, so `b`'s NaN wins over `a`'s.\n   327\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   328\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Mulvs(a: c2v, b: f32) -> c2v {\n   329\t    let mut a = a;\n   330\t    a.x = mul_r(a.x, b);\n   331\t    a.y = mul_r(a.y, b);\n   332\t    a\n   333\t}\n   334\t\n   335\t/// `c2v c2Maxv(c2v a, c2v b)`\n   336\t///\n   337\t/// GCC emits `maxss %xmm(b), %xmm(a)`, whose NaN behaviour (\"if either\n   338\t/// operand is NaN, return the source operand\") is exactly the C ternary\n   339\t/// `a > b ? a : b`. Do **not** use `f32::max` here — it returns the\n   340\t/// non-NaN operand instead.\n   341\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   342\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Maxv(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   343\t    c2V(\n   344\t        if a.x > b.x { a.x } else { b.x },\n   345\t        if a.y > b.y { a.y } else { b.y },\n   346\t    )\n   347\t}\n   348\t\n   349\t/// `c2v c2Minv(c2v a, c2v b)` — `minss`, mirroring `c2Maxv`.\n   350\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   351\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Minv(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   352\t    c2V(\n   353\t        if a.x < b.x { a.x } else { b.x },\n   354\t        if a.y < b.y { a.y } else { b.y },\n   355\t    )\n   356\t}\n   357\t\n   358\t/// `c2v c2Clampv(c2v a, c2v lo, c2v hi)`\n   359\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   360\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Clampv(a: c2v, lo: c2v, hi: c2v) -> c2v {\n   361\t    c2Maxv(lo, c2Minv(a, hi))\n   362\t}\n   363\t\n   364\t/// `c2v c2Sub(c2v a, c2v b)` — `subps %xmm1(b), %xmm0(a)`, destination `a`.\n   365\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   366\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Sub(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   367\t    let mut a = a;\n   368\t    a.x = sub_l(a.x, b.x);\n   369\t    a.y = sub_l(a.y, b.y);\n   370\t    a\n   371\t}\n   372\t\n   373\t/// `c2v c2Add(c2v a, c2v b)` — `addps %xmm1(b), %xmm0(a)`, destination `a`.\n   374\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   375\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Add(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   376\t    let mut a = a;\n   377\t    a.x = add_l(a.x, b.x);\n   378\t    a.y = add_l(a.y, b.y);\n   379\t    a\n   380\t}\n   381\t\n   382\t/// `float c2Dot(c2v a, c2v b)` -> `a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y`\n   383\t///\n   384\t/// ```text\n   385\t/// mulss %xmm1,%xmm0   ; xmm0 = a.x * b.x   (dst a.x)\n   386\t/// mulss %xmm3,%xmm1   ; xmm1 = a.y * b.y   (dst a.y)\n   387\t/// addss %xmm1,%xmm0   ; dst = a.x * b.x\n   388\t/// ```\n   389\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   390\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Dot(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> f32 {\n   391\t    add_l(mul_l(a.x, b.x), mul_l(a.y, b.y))\n   392\t}\n   393\t\n   394\t/// `float c2Det2(c2v a, c2v b)` -> `a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x`\n   395\t///\n   396\t/// ```text\n   397\t/// mulss %xmm2,%xmm0   ; xmm0 = a.x * b.y   (dst a.x)\n   398\t/// mulss %xmm3,%xmm1   ; xmm1 = a.y * b.x   (dst a.y)\n   399\t/// subss %xmm1,%xmm0   ; dst = a.x * b.y\n   400\t/// ```\n   401\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   402\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Det2(a: c2v, b: c2v) -> f32 {\n   403\t    sub_l(mul_l(a.x, b.y), mul_l(a.y, b.x))\n   404\t}\n   405\t\n   406\t/// `c2r c2RotIdentity(void)`\n   407\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   408\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2RotIdentity() -> c2r {\n   409\t    let mut r = c2r { c: 0.0, s: 0.0 };\n   410\t    r.c = 1.0f32;\n   411\t    r.s = 0.0;\n   412\t    r\n   413\t}\n   414\t\n   415\t/// `c2x c2xIdentity(void)`\n   416\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   417\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2xIdentity() -> c2x {\n   418\t    let mut x = c2x {\n   419\t        p: ZERO_V,\n   420\t        r: c2r { c: 0.0, s: 0.0 },\n   421\t    };\n   422\t    x.p = c2V(0.0, 0.0);\n   423\t    x.r = c2RotIdentity();\n   424\t    x\n   425\t}\n   426\t\n   427\t/// `c2v c2Mulrv(c2r a, c2v b)`\n   428\t/// -> `c2V(a.c * b.x - a.s * b.y, a.s * b.x + a.c * b.y)`\n   429\t///\n   430\t/// GCC commuted the `y` term's `addss`, making `a.c * b.y` the destination:\n   431\t///\n   432\t/// ```text\n   433\t/// mulss %xmm1,%xmm5   ; xmm5 = a.s * b.x   (dst a.s)\n   434\t/// mulss %xmm3,%xmm1   ; xmm1 = a.c * b.y   (dst a.c)\n   435\t/// mulss %xmm4,%xmm0   ; xmm0 = a.c * b.x   (dst a.c)\n   436\t/// mulss %xmm3,%xmm2   ; xmm2 = a.s * b.y   (dst a.s)\n   437\t/// addss %xmm5,%xmm1   ; y = (a.c*b.y) + (a.s*b.x)   <- dst is the 2nd term\n   438\t/// subss %xmm2,%xmm0   ; x = (a.c*b.x) - (a.s*b.y)\n   439\t/// ```\n   440\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   441\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Mulrv(a: c2r, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   442\t    c2V(\n   443\t        sub_l(mul_l(a.c, b.x), mul_l(a.s, b.y)),\n   444\t        add_r(mul_l(a.s, b.x), mul_l(a.c, b.y)),\n   445\t    )\n   446\t}\n   447\t\n   448\t/// `c2v c2MulrvT(c2r a, c2v b)`\n   449\t/// -> `c2V(a.c * b.x + a.s * b.y, -a.s * b.x + a.c * b.y)`\n   450\t///\n   451\t/// GCC folded `-x + y` into `subss` with `y` as the destination. IEEE-754\n   452\t/// addition is commutative, so `(-x) + y == y - x` bit-exactly for every\n   453\t/// non-NaN operand (signed zeros and infinities included):\n   454\t///\n   455\t/// ```text\n   456\t/// subss %xmm5,%xmm1   ; y = (a.c*b.y) - (a.s*b.x)\n   457\t/// addss %xmm2,%xmm0   ; x = (a.c*b.x) + (a.s*b.y)\n   458\t/// ```\n   459\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   460\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2MulrvT(a: c2r, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   461\t    c2V(\n   462\t        add_l(mul_l(a.c, b.x), mul_l(a.s, b.y)),\n   463\t        sub_l(mul_l(a.c, b.y), mul_l(a.s, b.x)),\n   464\t    )\n   465\t}\n   466\t\n   467\t/// `c2v c2Mulxv(c2x a, c2v b)`\n   468\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   469\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Mulxv(a: c2x, b: c2v) -> c2v {\n   470\t    c2Add(c2Mulrv(a.r, b), a.p)\n   471\t}\n   472\t\n   473\t/// `c2v c2Neg(c2v a)` — `xorps` sign flip, no NaN quieting.\n   474\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   475\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Neg(a: c2v) -> c2v {\n   476\t    c2V(fneg(a.x), fneg(a.y))\n   477\t}\n   478\t\n   479\t/// `c2v c2Skew(c2v a)`\n   480\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   481\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Skew(a: c2v) -> c2v {\n   482\t    let mut b = ZERO_V;\n   483\t    b.x = fneg(a.y);\n   484\t    b.y = a.x;\n   485\t    b\n   486\t}\n   487\t\n   488\t/// `c2v c2CCW90(c2v a)`\n   489\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   490\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2CCW90(a: c2v) -> c2v {\n   491\t    let mut b = ZERO_V;\n   492\t    b.x = a.y;\n   493\t    b.y = fneg(a.x);\n   494\t    b\n   495\t}\n   496\t\n   497\t/// `c2v c2Div(c2v a, float b)` — `divss %xmm1(b), %xmm2(1.0f)`.\n   498\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   499\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Div(a: c2v, b: f32) -> c2v {\n   500\t    c2Mulvs(a, div_l(1.0f32, b))\n   501\t}\n   502\t\n   503\t/// `c2v c2Norm(c2v a)`\n   504\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   505\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Norm(a: c2v) -> c2v {\n   506\t    c2Div(a, c2Len(a))\n   507\t}\n   508\t\n   509\t/// `float c2Len(c2v a)` -> `sqrtf(c2Dot(a, a))`\n   510\t///\n   511\t/// GCC guards the inline `sqrtss` with `ucomiss`/`ja` and falls back to\n   512\t/// `sqrtf@plt` only when the argument is *strictly negative* (NaN leaves the\n   513\t/// branch untaken, because `ja` is false when unordered). The argument here is\n   514\t/// always a sum of squares, so it is never negative and the inline `sqrtss` —\n   515\t/// i.e. `f32::sqrt` — is always what runs.\n   516\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   517\tpub extern \"C\" fn c2Len(a: c2v) -> f32 {\n   518\t    c2Dot(a, a).sqrt()\n   519\t}\n   520\t\n   521\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   522\t// Shape helpers\n   523\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   524\t\n   525\t/// `void c2BBVerts(c2v *out, c2AABB *bb)`\n   526\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   527\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn c2BBVerts(out: *mut c2v, bb: *mut c2AABB) {\n   528\t    unsafe {\n   529\t        *out.add(0) = (*bb).min;\n   530\t        *out.add(1) = c2V((*bb).max.x, (*bb).min.y);\n   531\t        *out.add(2) = (*bb).max;\n   532\t        *out.add(3) = c2V((*bb).min.x, (*bb).max.y);\n   533\t    }\n   534\t}\n   535\t\n   536\t/// `void c2MakeProxy(const void *shape, C2_TYPE type, c2Proxy *p)`\n   537\t///\n   538\t/// As in the C original there is no `default:` arm, so for a `type` outside\n   539\t/// `{0, 1, 2}` the proxy is left completely untouched.\n   540\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n   541\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn c2MakeProxy(shape: *const c_void, type_: c_int, p: *mut c2Proxy) {\n   542\t    unsafe {\n   543\t        match type_ {\n   544\t            C2_TYPE_CIRCLE => {\n   545\t                let c = shape as *mut c2Circle;\n   546\t                (*p).radius = (*c).r;\n   547\t                (*p).count = 1;\n   548\t                (*p).verts[0] = (*c).p;\n   549\t            }\n   550\t            C2_TYPE_AABB => {\n   551\t                let bb = shape as *mut c2AABB;\n   552\t                (*p).radius = 0.0;\n   553\t                (*p).count = 4;\n   554\t                c2BBVerts(addr_of_mut!((*p).verts) as *mut c2v, bb);\n   555\t            }\n   556\t            C2_TYPE_CAPSULE => {\n   557\t                let c =